<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Mutation rules for `just mutants` (CLAUDE.md §3).

  Coverage answers "did this line run". Mutation answers "would any assertion
  notice if this line changed". Only the second one catches a test that calls a
  function and asserts nothing useful about the result.

  THIS DOCUMENT IS THE WHOLE RULE SET. `-b` is NOT passed any more, and that
  reversal is the point of the M1 remediation.

  It used to say: "the builtin rule set is kept (`-b`); rules themselves are not
  customised, so an upstream improvement to them arrives for free." True of the
  RULES. But `-b` also adds mutation_test's builtin **exclusions**, and one of
  those is a hole:

      <regex pattern="[\s]for[\s]*\(.*?\)[\s]*{" dotAll="true"/>

  A Dart COLLECTION-for has no `{` to stop at, so `.*?` runs past the end of the
  loop hunting for one and swallows everything up to the next function's opening
  brace. Measured on `lib/src/resume/engine.dart`: **lines 128-171, 1,959
  characters** — the rest of `deriveView` plus `setWatched`, `clearWatched`,
  `clearProgress` and `setFavorite`. The proof is in the mutant list, not the
  regex: mutated lines ran 8, 24, 26, 48, 50, 76-99, 121, 122, then jumped
  straight to 172. `i < pointerIndex`, which decides whether the file AT the
  pointer is marked watched, was never once mutated. "149 of 149 killed" was
  measured over a source file with a 44-line blind spot in it.

  Tightening our own copy of the pattern did nothing, because `-b` re-added the
  loose one — verified: engine.dart produced an identical 62-mutation list under
  the old rules, the tightened rules, and with our loop exclusions deleted
  outright. Additive exclusions cannot be subtracted. So the builtin rules below
  are transcribed VERBATIM from `dart run mutation_test -g` at the pinned 1.7.1,
  `-b` is dropped, and the exclusions are ours alone.

  The cost is real and is the price of closing the hole: an upstream rule
  improvement no longer arrives for free. RETIREMENT CONDITION — restore `-b`
  and delete the transcribed block the day mutation_test's builtin `for`
  exclusion cannot swallow a collection-for. Re-transcribe on every
  mutation_test bump; the pin in `pubspec.yaml` is what makes that a decision
  rather than a surprise.

  THRESHOLD IS 100. Anything less means "some mutants may survive", and a
  surviving mutant is a change to our code that no test objects to. The gate is
  diff-scoped, so 100% is asking about the handful of mutants this commit adds,
  not the whole tree.
-->
<mutations version="1.1">
  <!--
    THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO <commands> BLOCK HERE, AND tool/check-mutants.sh
    FAILS IF ONE COMES BACK.

    The test command is per PACKAGE — `dart test` for `packages/*`, `flutter
    test --no-pub` for `apps/*` — so it is written into the targets document
    that script generates, which knows which package it is building.

    A block here would not be an overridable default. Measured at M3.0 against
    the pinned 1.7.1: a `<commands>` in the rules document runs IN ADDITION to
    a `<commands>` in the targets document. With `dart test` here and `flutter
    test` there, `dart test` still ran inside `apps/mobile` and still failed.

    What that failure looked like is recorded because it was milder than
    predicted: mutation_test runs the command set against unmodified code
    first, so a command that always fails ABORTS the run ("Running the test
    commands failed with unmodified code! Aborting.", rc 1) instead of marking
    every mutant detected. Fail-closed, not a silent 100% over nothing. The
    refusal in check-mutants.sh stands anyway — the gate could not run on the
    app at all, and the next global command someone adds may fail less
    honestly.

    RETIREMENT CONDITION: none. The command belongs with the target.
  -->

  <!--
    THE RULES BLOCK BELOW IS IN TWO HALVES.

    First, mutation_test 1.7.1's builtin set, transcribed verbatim from
    `dart run mutation_test -g` — comments included, so a future diff against a
    newer `-g` dump is a plain text diff. Do not edit it by hand; regenerate it.
    See the header for why it is here at all rather than being had for free.

    Second, our two ADDED RULES.

    The builtin set mutates `<=` and `>=` (to `==` and to the strict form) but
    has NO rule for a bare `<` or `>`. So a STRICT comparison is never weakened
    to a non-strict one, and the off-by-one at a boundary — the single most
    common comparison bug, and the one §3 cites mutation testing for — was
    invisible to this gate. Measured at M1.9 on `decide()`: with every
    equal-to-threshold assertion deleted from `decide_test.dart`, changing
    `file.size > settings.meteredWarnBytes` to `>=` passed the suite, and
    `just mutants` still reported 6 of 6 killed. It was not asking.

    The patterns require whitespace on BOTH sides, which is what keeps them off
    everything that is not a comparison: `List<bool>` and `Map<String, int>`
    have no space before `>` or after `<`, `=>` has `=` immediately before the
    `>`, and `<=` / `>=` have `=` immediately after. Verified against this
    package's own sources before being added.

    Never retires: the operators are not going away.
  -->
  <rules>
    <!-- A literal element matches the literal text and replaces it with the list of mutations  -->
    <!-- Replaces 'and' and 'or'  with each other -->
    <literal text="&amp;&amp;" id="builtin.and">
      <mutation text="||"/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="||" id="builtin.or">
      <mutation text="&amp;&amp;"/>
    </literal>
    <!-- Replaces assignments with other assignments -->
    <literal text="+=" id="builtin.op.add_assign">
      <mutation text="="/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="-=" id="builtin.op.sub_assign">
      <mutation text="="/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="*=" id="builtin.op.mul_assign">
      <mutation text="="/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="/=" id="builtin.op.div_assign">
      <mutation text="="/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="&=" id="builtin.op.and_assign">
      <mutation text="="/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="^=" id="builtin.op.or_assign">
      <mutation text="="/>
    </literal>
    <!-- Replaces comparison operators -->
    <literal text="==" id="builtin.op.eq">
      <mutation text="!="/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="!=" id="builtin.op.neq">
      <mutation text="=="/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="&lt;=" id="builtin.op.leq">
      <mutation text="=="/>
      <mutation text="&lt;"/>
    </literal>
    <literal text="&gt;=" id="builtin.op.geq">
      <mutation text="=="/>
      <mutation text="&gt;"/>
    </literal>
    <!-- It is also possible to match a regular expression with capture groups. -->
    <!-- If the optional attribute dotAll is set to true, then the . will also match newlines.  -->
    <!-- If not present, the default value for dotAll is false.  -->
    <!-- Here, we capture everything inside of the braces of "if ()" -->
    <regex pattern="[\s]if[\s]*\((.*?)\)[\s]*{" dotAll="true" id="builtin.if">
      <!-- You can access groups via $1. -->
      <!-- If your string contains a $ followed by a number that should not be replaced, escape the dollar \$ -->
      <!-- If your string contains a \$ followed by a number that should not be replaced, escape the slash \\$ -->
      <!-- Tabs and newlines should also be escaped. -->
      <mutation text=" if (!($1)) {"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Matches long chains of && -->
    <regex pattern="&amp;([^&amp;()]+?)&amp;" dotAll="true" id="builtin.logical.and_chain">
      <mutation text="&amp;!($1)&amp;"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Matches long chains of || -->
    <regex pattern="\|([^|()]+?)\|" dotAll="true" id="builtin.logical.or_chain">
      <mutation text="|!($1)|"/>
    </regex>
    <regex pattern="\(([^$(]*?)&amp;&amp;([^$()]*?)\)" id="builtin.logical.and_chain2">
      <mutation text="(!($1)&amp;&amp;$2)"/>
      <mutation text="($1&amp;&amp;!($2))"/>
    </regex>
    <regex pattern="\(([^|(]*?)\|\|([^()|]*?)\)" id="builtin.logical.or_chain2">
      <mutation text="(!($1)||$2)"/>
      <mutation text="($1||!($2))"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Replace start of conditional block -->
    <regex pattern="if\s*\(([^|&amp;\)]*?)([|&amp;][|&amp;])" id="builtin.if.start">
      <mutation text="if (!($1)$2"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Replace end of conditional block -->
    <regex pattern="([|&amp;][|&amp;])([^|&amp;]*?)\)" id="builtin.if.end">
      <mutation text="$1!($2))"/>
    </regex>
    <regex pattern="([|&amp;][|&amp;])[\s]*?\(" dotAll="true" id="builtin.logical.chain_not">
      <mutation text="$1!("/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Replaces numbers with negative values -->
    <regex pattern="([\s=\(])([1-9\.]+[0-9]+|0\.0*[1-9])" id="builtin.number.negative">
      <mutation text="$1-$2"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- checks if neighboring arguments may have been mixed up -->
    <!-- switch function call arguments. Matches 2 args -->
    <regex pattern="([\s][a-zA-Z]+?[^(;\s{}]*?)\s*\(([^,:;{}(]+?),([^,:;{}(]+?)\)\s*;" id="builtin.function.arg2">
      <mutation text="$1($3,$2);"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- switch function call arguments. Matches 3 args -->
    <regex pattern="([\s][a-zA-Z]+?[^\(;\s{}]*?)\s*\(([^,:;{}(]+?),([^,:;{}(]+?),([^,:;{}(]+?)\)\s*;" id="builtin.function.arg3">
      <mutation text="$1($3,$2,$4);"/>
      <mutation text="$1($2,$4,$3);"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- switch function call arguments. Matches 4 args -->
    <regex pattern="([\s][a-zA-Z]+?[^\(;\s{}]*?)\s*\(([^,:;{}(]+?),([^,:;{}(]+?),([^,:;{}(]+?),([^,:;{}(]+?)\)\s*;"  id="builtin.function.arg4">
      <mutation text="$1($3,$2,$4,$5);"/>
      <mutation text="$1($2,$4,$3,$5);"/>
      <mutation text="$1($2,$3,$5,$4);"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Replaces arithmetic operators with their opposite -->
    <regex pattern="\+([^=])" id="builtin.arith.add">
      <mutation text="-$1"/>
    </regex>
    <regex pattern="-([^=])" id="builtin.arith.sub">
      <mutation text="+$1"/>
    </regex>
    <regex pattern="\*([^=])" id="builtin.arith.mul">
      <mutation text="/$1"/>
    </regex>
    <regex pattern="/([^=])" id="builtin.arith.div">
      <mutation text="*$1"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Removes break; statements for c++ switches -->
    <regex pattern="break;(\s+)case" id="builtin.switch.break">
      <mutation text="$1case"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Removes entries in a list in dart -->
    <regex pattern="([=:>]\s*)\[([^\],]+),([^\]]+)\]" id="builtin.list.clear">
      <mutation text="$1[]"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- Removes function calls that are not returned or assigned to a value -->
    <regex pattern="([{]\s*)([^\(;=\s}]+\([^;]+\)\s*;)" id="builtin.function.removeVoidCall1">
      <mutation text="$1"/>
    </regex>
    <regex pattern="([};]\s*)([^\(;=\s}]+\([^;]+\)\s*;)" id="builtin.function.removeVoidCall2">
      <mutation text="$1"/>
    </regex>
    <!-- ==== END of the verbatim `mutation_test -g` transcription ==== -->

    <!-- ==== OURS ==== -->
    <regex pattern="(\s)&lt;(\s)" id="filefin.op.lt">
      <mutation text="$1&lt;=$2"/>
    </regex>
    <regex pattern="(\s)&gt;(\s)" id="filefin.op.gt">
      <mutation text="$1&gt;=$2"/>
    </regex>
  </rules>

  <!--
    Exclusions. Each needs a reason and a retirement condition, because an
    exclusion is a piece of code the gate has stopped asking about.
  -->
  <exclude>
    <!-- Comments carry no behaviour, so mutating them produces mutants no test
         can ever kill. Never retires. -->
    <token begin="//" end="\n"/>
    <regex pattern="/[*].*?[*]/" dotAll="true"/>

    <!-- Import and export directives: mutating a URI produces a file that does
         not compile, which is an unviable mutant, not a missing assertion.
         Never retires. -->
    <token begin="import &apos;" end="&apos;;"/>
    <token begin="export &apos;" end="&apos;;"/>
    <token begin="import &quot;" end="&quot;;"/>
    <token begin="export &quot;" end="&quot;;"/>

    <!-- `part` / `part of` directives bind a library to its generated code.
         Same reasoning as imports. Never retires. -->
    <token begin="part &apos;" end="&apos;;"/>

    <!-- Loops: the builtin rules invert loop conditions, which turns a bounded
         loop into an unbounded one and hangs the run rather than reporting a
         survivor. Retires if mutation_test gains a per-mutant kill that can
         distinguish a hang from a survivor reliably; the 300s command timeout
         is the current mitigation and it costs 300s per such mutant.

         THE HEADER IS `[^)]*`, NOT `.*?` WITH dotAll, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
         POINT — see the document header for the measurement. `[^)]*` cannot
         cross a `)`, so the match either closes on this loop's own header or
         does not happen at all, and a braceless collection-for can no longer
         swallow the rest of the file. On `engine.dart`: the old pattern
         matched once for 1,959 characters, this one matches nothing; on a
         file with a real `for (…) {`, this one still excludes that header.

         The cost, stated rather than discovered later: a `for` whose header
         contains a call with parentheses is no longer excluded and could hang
         the run against the 300s timeout. That is the direction to fail in,
         and it is louder than a silent 44-line hole. -->
    <regex pattern="[\s]for[\s]*\([^)]*\)[\s]*{"/>
    <regex pattern="[\s]while[\s]*\([^)]*\)[\s]*{.*?}" dotAll="true"/>

    <!-- The two genuinely equivalent mutants the `filefin.op.lt`/`.gt` rules
         above produce, each excluded by its exact text rather than by line
         number, so an edit elsewhere cannot silently widen the exclusion.

         1. `if (x < 0) return 0;` in the resume engine's rounding. `<=` differs
            only at x == 0, and there both branches produce 0 —
            `(0.0 + 0.5).toInt()` is 0. Clamping AT zero is equivalent to
            clamping BELOW zero for every possible input; no test can separate
            them because no behaviour separates them.

         2. `targetSeconds > state.pointer!.seconds` in applyProgress. `>=`
            differs only when the two are equal, and in that case the branch
            writes `ResumePointer(targetIndex, targetSeconds)` over a pointer
            that already holds exactly those two values. WatchState compares by
            value, so the state before and after are equal.

         Both retire if the surrounding expression changes shape — the patterns
         are exact, so they simply stop matching and the mutants come back.
         Verified equivalent by hand at M1.9 before excluding, not assumed. -->
    <regex pattern="if \(x &lt; 0\) return 0;"/>
    <regex pattern="targetSeconds &gt; state\.pointer!\.seconds"/>

    <!-- ++ and -- : the builtin rule rewrites them into each other via the
         arithmetic rules and produces mostly false positives on index maths.
         Retires when the resume engine's index arithmetic is expressed without
         them (it currently is — this is inherited from the builtin default and
         may be dropped at M1.9 if no survivor depends on it). -->
    <regex pattern="\+\+"/>
    <regex pattern="--"/>
  </exclude>

  <threshold failure="100">
    <rating over="99.9" name="A"/>
    <rating over="90" name="B"/>
    <rating over="75" name="C"/>
    <rating over="50" name="D"/>
    <rating over="0" name="F"/>
  </threshold>
</mutations>
